    title: "Continental Drift & Plate Tectonics",
    text: "Continental drift and plate tectonics are fundamental theories in geology that describe the large-scale movements of Earth's lithosphere. In 1912, the German meteorologist Alfred Wegener proposed the theory of continental drift. He hypothesized that all the continents were once assembled into a single supercontinent, which he named Pangaea, meaning 'All-Earth' in Greek. According to Wegener's model, Pangaea began to break apart during the Mesozoic era, around 200 million years ago, and the fragments slowly drifted to their current positions. To support his hypothesis, Wegener pointed to the striking jigsaw-like fit of continental coastlines, such as South America and Africa, as well as identical fossil remains of ancient plants and animals found on widely separated landmasses. Despite this compelling circumstantial evidence, Wegener's theory was widely rejected by the contemporary scientific community, primarily because he could not provide a plausible physical mechanism to explain how solid continents could plow through the ocean floor.\n\nThe breakthrough came in the mid-20th century with the development of oceanography and the discovery of the mid-ocean ridge system. Scientists mapped the deep ocean floor and discovered seafloor spreading, a process where molten magma rises from the Earth's mantle at mid-ocean ridges, cools to form new crust, and pushes the existing tectonic plates apart. This discovery led to the modern theory of plate tectonics, which synthesized Wegener's continental drift with seafloor spreading. Plate tectonics posits that the Earth's outer shell, or lithosphere, is divided into several rigid plates that float on the hot, semi-fluid asthenosphere below. The movement of these plates is driven by mantle convection, where heat from the Earth's interior creates convection currents that slowly move the lithospheric plates. Today, plate tectonics explains not only the movement of continents but also the formation of major geological features, such as mountain ranges, volcanic arcs, and deep-ocean trenches, as well as the occurrence of earthquakes along active plate boundaries.",
