    text: "Discovered in 1977 during an expedition by the research submersible Alvin near the Galapagos Rift, deep-sea hydrothermal vents are fissures on the seafloor that release geothermally heated water rich in dissolved minerals. These vents typically form along mid-ocean ridges, where tectonic plates are pulling apart and magma rises close to the ocean floor. Cold seawater penetrates deep into the Earth's crust through cracks, where it is superheated by underlying magma to temperatures exceeding 400 degrees Celsius. Despite the extreme heat, the water does not boil due to the immense hydrostatic pressure of the deep ocean. As this superheated fluid rises back up, it carries dissolved metals and sulfide minerals, which precipitate out upon contact with near-freezing ocean water, creating chimney-like towers.\n\nUnlike surface ecosystems that depend on solar energy and photosynthesis, the unique biological communities surrounding these vents rely on chemosynthesis. In this process, specialized chemosynthetic bacteria serve as the primary producers. These bacteria extract energy from toxic chemical compounds, specifically hydrogen sulfide, which is abundant in the vent fluids. They convert this chemical energy into organic matter, supporting a dense and diverse array of deep-sea life. Many vent organisms, such as giant tubeworms (Riftia pachyptila), have evolved symbiotic relationships with these bacteria, housing them inside specialized organs. Other creatures, including unique species of crabs, shrimp, and snails, feed directly on bacterial mats, demonstrating that complex life can thrive in the complete absence of sunlight.",
