    title: "Development of Agriculture",
    text: "The transition of human societies from nomadic foraging to settled agriculture during the Neolithic Revolution, beginning around 10,000 BC, represents one of the most profound turning points in human history. Prior to this epoch, humans lived in small, highly mobile bands that relied entirely on hunting wild animals and gathering wild plants. By starting to domesticate wild crops like wheat, barley, and rice, and animals such as sheep, goats, and cattle, human groups gained control over their food supply, transitioning from opportunistic gatherers to active producers of nutrients.\n\nThis agricultural transition triggered a cascade of fundamental social changes. For the first time, societies could produce food surpluses, which allowed populations to grow exponentially and congregate in permanent, densely populated settlements. With the food supply secured, not every individual was required to farm, leading to the division of labor. Specialized occupations emerged, including artisans, warriors, administrators, and priests. To keep track of agricultural grain storage, trade transactions, and property boundaries, early civilizations developed the first writing systems. This structural expansion laid the organizational groundwork for the rise of complex city-states and the earliest modern human civilizations.",
