  {
    title: "Earthquakes and Seismic Waves",
    text: "Earthquakes are sudden, violent shaking movements of the Earth's surface caused by the abrupt release of accumulated energy in the lithosphere. This geological phenomenon typically occurs along plate boundaries, where tectonic plates grind past one another. Due to intense friction, the plates often become locked, preventing smooth movement. However, the forces driving tectonic plates do not stop, causing mechanical stress to accumulate along the locked fault lines over decades or centuries. When the accumulated stress finally exceeds the frictional resistance, the rock ruptures, slipping violently in an event known as elastic rebound, which releases energy in all directions.\n\nThis released energy propagates outward from the origin point—the focus or hypocenter—through the Earth's crust as seismic waves. Seismologists classify these into body waves and surface waves. Body waves travel through the interior of the Earth and include primary waves (P-waves), which are fast, compressional waves that move rocks forward and backward, and secondary waves (S-waves), which are slower, shear waves that shake rock side-to-side and can only travel through solids. Surface waves, which travel along the Earth's exterior, are the slowest seismic waves but cause the most violent ground displacement and destruction. Seismologists record these waves using sensitive instruments called seismographs, allowing them to determine the earthquake's epicenter on the surface and calculate its magnitude using modern logarithmic scales.",
    questions: [
      { id: 1, question: "What causes the sudden release of energy during an earthquake?", options: ["Volcanic magma cooling", "Accumulated stress exceeding frictional resistance along faults", "The gravitational pull of the moon", "High atmospheric pressure"], correct: "Accumulated stress exceeding frictional resistance along faults", hint: "Check the fourth sentence of the first paragraph." },
      { id: 2, question: "What is the theory that explains how rocks store and release energy during earthquakes?", options: ["Plate tectonics", "The geodynamo", "Elastic rebound theory", "Bernoulli's theory"], correct: "Elastic rebound theory", hint: "Check the final sentence of the first paragraph." },
      { id: 3, question: "What is the actual point of rupture deep inside the Earth called?", options: ["The epicenter", "The focus or hypocenter", "The fault line", "The asthenosphere"], correct: "The focus or hypocenter", hint: "Check the first sentence of the second paragraph." },
      { id: 4, question: "What are the two major classifications of seismic waves?", options: ["Body waves and surface waves", "P-waves and S-waves only", "Compressional and shear waves only", "Oceanic and continental waves"], correct: "Body waves and surface waves", hint: "Check the second sentence of the second paragraph." },
      { id: 5, question: "Which seismic waves are the fastest and arrive first?", options: ["Surface waves", "S-waves", "Primary waves (P-waves)", "Tsunami waves"], correct: "Primary waves (P-waves)", hint: "Check the third sentence of the second paragraph." },
      { id: 6, question: "What is a major limitation of secondary waves (S-waves)?", options: ["They cannot travel through solid rock", "They can only travel through solid materials", "They travel faster than light", "They do not register on seismographs"], correct: "They can only travel through solid materials", hint: "Check the third sentence of the second paragraph." },
      { id: 7, question: "Which type of seismic waves causes the most surface destruction?", options: ["P-waves", "S-waves", "Surface waves", "Body waves"], correct: "Surface waves", hint: "Check the fourth sentence of the second paragraph." },
      { id: 8, question: "What instrument is used to measure and record seismic waves?", options: ["A barometer", "A seismograph", "A cartouche", "A spectrograph"], correct: "A seismograph", hint: "Check the fifth sentence of the second paragraph." },
      { id: 9, question: "What is the point on the Earth's surface directly above the earthquake's origin called?", options: ["The focus", "The epicenter", "The fault line", "The asthenosphere"], correct: "The epicenter", hint: "Check the final sentence of the passage." },
      { id: 10, question: "How do P-waves physically move rocks?", options: ["Side-to-side in a shearing motion", "In a circular ocean-like wave", "Forward and backward in a compressional motion", "They melt the rocks completely"], correct: "Forward and backward in a compressional motion", hint: "Check the third sentence of the second paragraph." }
    ]
  }
